<div class="wrap page prose">
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>How to read a profile</h1>
    <p class="lede">The site displays the approved research record. It does not rescore vendors, fill gaps, or turn a screening note into a legal clearance.</p>
  </header>

  <h2>What is published</h2>
  <p>Each listed vendor is one YAML file using schema 1.2. The field guide is in <code>data/research/schema.md</code>. A file is listed only when <code>identity.signoff</code> is <code>APPROVED</code> and <code>meta.signoff_status</code> is <code>approved</code>. <code>meta.last_reviewed</code> is shown on the profile and in the comparison table.</p>
  <p>Fictional example profiles stay in <code>data/examples/</code>. They are not in the overview, the comparison table, or the use-case pages.</p>

  <h2>Scores</h2>
  <p>Major sections carry a dimension score from 0 to 5, a confidence badge (high, medium, or low), a written rationale, source URLs, and a last-verified date. Those values are copied from the file. Use-case fit uses the same pattern for the personas stored on the profile: LE investigator, corporate SOC, brand / PR, due diligence, and GEOINT analyst.</p>
  <p>There is no universal ranking. Sorting the table by a score only changes the order of the recorded numbers.</p>

  <h2>Sources and unknowns</h2>
  <p>A claim that has a source URL shows that URL beside the claim. The bottom of the profile lists every http(s) source stored on the file. Where the file says unknown, or none publicly documented, the page shows that wording. Missing sections say they were not recorded in the profile.</p>

  <h2>Red flags</h2>
  <p>Red flags are the checklist strings in <code>red_flags</code>. They are shown in full and counted in the comparison table. An empty list is “no red flags recorded in this profile,” not a finding that none exist.</p>

  <h2>Jurisdiction and sanctions exposure</h2>
  <p>The page shows the company-level record: offices and subsidiaries, ownership and investor ties, aggregate workforce signals, and the EU, UN, OFAC, and UK screening lines with the screening date, method, names checked, and sources. The result sentence is shown as written. “No public hit identified” still includes the file’s re-screen caveat. “Review required” is shown when that is the recorded result, including any list strings stored on the profile.</p>
  <p>This is not a sanctions determination and not legal advice.</p>

  <h2>Known customers</h2>
  <p>Customers appear only when the file status is <code>documented</code>. Each row shows the name, sector, country, evidence type, date, notes, and source URL from the file. Status <code>none_publicly_documented</code> is shown as none publicly documented, with the research note if one was recorded. The comparison page counts those public records by sector. A zero is not proof that a vendor has no buyers in that sector.</p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>Open an issue or pull request on the source repository. Name the claim and the better public source. Do not send confidential client lists or personal data about employees.</p>
</div>
